"Shows one actor's actions, thoughts and feelings while pursuing a particular goal across relevant touchpoints.\n\nUse when: Understand friction across an end-to-end customer scenario. Prioritize experience improvements using research evidence.\n\nAvoid when: The main need is staff responsibilities or backstage service execution.\n\nVisual grammar: Journey phases organize actions in temporal order. Lanes record the actor's behavior, mindset and emotional evidence. Opportunity notes identify possible improvements without presenting them as observed facts.\n\nBuild: Choose one actor, goal, scenario and experience boundary. Synthesize interviews and observations into relevant phases and moments. Identify pain points and opportunities, retaining evidence links and uncertainty.\n\nCheck: All lanes refer to the same actor and scenario. Emotional claims are supported by research or labeled assumptions. Current-state and future-state experiences are not silently mixed.\n\nPitfalls: Combining several incompatible personas into an average journey. Inventing an emotional curve for visual drama.\n\nExample: A first-time visitor researches a museum, buys a ticket, finds the entrance and explores the galleries, exposing an arrival information gap.\n\nAlternatives: Experience map, Service blueprint."
